  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1129 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nTuesday night through Sunday:\n\nModels and ensembles suggest high pressure should move east\nacross the Western Great Lakes region Tuesday night into\nWednesday. The northeast winds with the high may limit any\nchances for showers across the area during this time. Ensemble\nmembers are mostly still showing measurable rainfall, but they\nmay not be seeing this drier trend yet. For now, kept chances \nfor showers (30 to 70 percent) for mainly Wednesday, but these \nmay need to be cut back in later forecasts if this drier look \ncontinues.\n\nSome warm air advection tries to sneak back into the area\nWednesday night into Thursday, though it is not very strong and\nmay lack moisture if another high to the north shunts any\nprecipitation with this system further to the south. Ensemble\nmembers continue to show measurable rainfall for this period,\nbut may not be catching onto this possible drier trend. For \nnow, kept the relatively high chances (50 to 80 percent) for \nshowers and a few storms for now. This may also need adjusting \ndownward in later forecasts. \n\nA more organized low tracks through southern Wisconsin or\nnorthern Illinois Saturday, with possibly another system for\nlater in the weekend. Ensemble members remain fairly wet with\nmeasurable rainfall during this period, so will keep the 30 to\n70 percent chances for now. Temperatures through the week and\ninto the weekend will depend on clouds and precipitaion trends,\nso kept NBM values for now until better confidence can be found. \n\nWood",
